)}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538091 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4148d41-ff0c-544e-a801-c54fea1c172d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538091 #edb/ref 1074 "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =- 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3 u \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =4 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =- 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & 3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & - 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ 3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ 4 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & - 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & - 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ 3 u \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)} & - 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} \\\\ 3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\sin{\\left(w \\right)} & 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(w \\right)}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(36 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos^2 w+\\sin^2 w\\right)+\\left(36 u^{2} \\sin^{3}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos^2 w+\\sin^2 w\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(36 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(36 u^{2} \\sin^{3}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =36 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\left(\\cos^2 v+\\sin^2 v\\right) \\\\  & =36 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538106 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186129271.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538106 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "44ca30fc-f158-595a-b275-1ead51c786d0" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549253 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549285 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549253 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549253 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))$, given that\n\n$$\nu=- 8 x\\qquad v=y\\qquad w=z.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549253 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13892e3f-d765-51b3-b960-59a3b31629f7" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549253 #edb/ref 1074 "For a transformation\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T$ is $\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}$.\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}$, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}=-8 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}=1 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-8\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-8\\left(1\\cdot 1-0\\right) \\\\ &=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549258 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549270 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549258 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549258 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2\\left(xy\\right) \\\\ yz \\\\ xz\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x,y,z>0.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549258 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28f499ce-a5b7-5f3f-90af-72bf76f6b98e" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549258 #edb/ref 1074 "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=2\\left(xy\\right),\\,v(x,y,z)=yz,\\,w(x,y,z)=xz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2\\left(y\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =2\\left(x\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nScaling the three derivative rows by $2, 1, 1$ multiplies the determinant by $2$.\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2\\left(y\\right) & 2\\left(x\\right) & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\left(y\\begin{vmatrix}z & y \\\\ 0 & x\\end{vmatrix}-x\\begin{vmatrix}0 & y \\\\ z & x\\end{vmatrix}\\right)\\\\  & =2\\left(y(xz-0)-x(0-yz)\\right)\\\\  & =2\\left(xyz+xyz\\right)\\\\  & =4 x y z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\left(2xyz\\right) & =2\\left(2\\sqrt{(xyz)^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(2\\sqrt{xy(yz)(xz)}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\left(2\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)vw}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4 \\sqrt{u v w}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549263 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549259 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549263 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549263 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))$, given that\n\n$$\nu=- 2 x\\qquad v=y\\qquad w=z.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549263 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3fd142b6-9fe1-551e-8de2-205d9c2a38c6" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549263 #edb/ref 1074 "For a transformation\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T$ is $\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}$.\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}$, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}=-2 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}=1 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=-2\\left(1\\cdot 1-0\\right) \\\\ &=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549274 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549294 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549274 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549274 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))$, given that\n\n$$\nu=6 x\\qquad v=y\\qquad w=z.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549274 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "911ff483-5437-5b63-bdea-c53a9f75354d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549274 #edb/ref 1074 "For a transformation\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T$ is $\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}$.\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}$, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}=6 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}=1 & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y}=0 & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=6\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=6\\left(1\\cdot 1-0\\right) \\\\ &=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}&=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549277 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549286 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549277 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549277 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3\\left({e}^{x}\\right) \\\\ 3\\left({e}^{x+y}\\right) \\\\ 2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549277 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4d85b42-ead4-58c7-a443-af6077a7bc6a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549277 #edb/ref 1074 "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=3\\left({e}^{x}\\right),\\,v(x,y,z)=3\\left({e}^{x+y}\\right),\\,w(x,y,z)=2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =3\\left({e}^{x}\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =3\\left({e}^{x+y}\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3\\left({e}^{x+y}\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right) & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nScaling the three derivative rows by $3, 3, 2$ multiplies the determinant by $18$.\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3\\left({e}^{x}\\right) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3\\left({e}^{x+y}\\right) & 3\\left({e}^{x+y}\\right) & 0 \\\\ 2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right) & 2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right) & 2\\left({e}^{x+y+z}\\right)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =18\\left({e}^{x}\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x+y} & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{vmatrix}\\right)\\\\  & =18\\left({e}^{x}({e}^{2x+2y+z}-0)\\right)\\\\  & =18 e^{x} e^{x + y} e^{x + y + z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18\\left({e}^{3x+2y+z}\\right) & =18\\left({e}^{x+(x+y)+(x+y+z)}\\right) \\\\  & =18\\left({e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x+y+z}\\right) \\\\  & =18\\left(\\left(\\frac{u}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{v}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{w}{2}\\right)\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u v w}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549282 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549255 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549282 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549282 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))$, given that\n\n$$\nu=- 4 x\\qquad v=y\\qquad w=z.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549282 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b767531b-eb1e-5afa-bf86-ac2bd324f05f" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549282 #edb/ref 1074 "For a transformation\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\to(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T$ is $\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}$.\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian